Article 6 Where a taxpayer falls under any of the following circumstances, the generation amount of taxable solid wastes in the current period shall be deemed as the discharge amount of solid wastes:
(1) illegally dumping taxable solid wastes;
(2) making false tax declarations.

Article 7 The tax basis for taxable air pollutants and water pollutants shall be determined based on the pollution equivalent numbers converted from the discharge amount of pollutants.
Where a taxpayer falls under any of the following circumstances, the generation amount of taxable air pollutants or water pollutants in the current period shall be deemed as the discharge amount of pollutants:
(1) failing to install and use automatic pollutant monitoring equipment according to law, or failing to connect the automatic pollutant monitoring equipment to the monitoring equipment of the competent environmental protection department;
(2) damaging, or without authorization moving or altering the automatic pollutant monitoring equipment;
(3) tampering with or falsifying pollutant monitoring data;
(4) illegally discharging taxable pollutants through concealed pipes, seepage wells, seepage pits, injection or dilution, or by not operating pollution prevention facilities normally, or through other means;
(5) making false tax declarations.

The competent department of environmental protection shall, through the tax-related information sharing platform, deliver the following information obtained from environmental protection supervision and management to the tax authorities:
(1) Basic information such as the name, unified social credit code, pollutant discharge outlets, and types of pollutants discharged by the pollutant discharging entity;
(2) Pollutant discharge data of the pollutant discharging entity (including data such as the amount of pollutants discharged and the concentration of air pollutants and water pollutants);
(3) Environmental violations and administrative penalties received by the pollutant discharging entity;
(4) Review opinions on abnormalities in the tax declaration data submitted by taxpayers that the tax authorities request for review or on taxpayers failing to make tax declarations within the prescribed time limit;
(5) Other information agreed with the tax authorities to be delivered.

The tax authorities shall, through the tax-related information sharing platform, deliver the following environmental protection tax-related information to the competent department of environmental protection:
(1) Basic information of taxpayers;
(2) Tax declaration information;
(3) Information such as tax payment into the treasury, tax reduction and exemption, overdue tax payments, and risk points;
(4) Tax-related violations and administrative penalties received by taxpayers;
(5) Information on abnormalities in the tax declaration data submitted by taxpayers or on taxpayers failing to make tax declarations within the prescribed time limit;
(6) Other information agreed with the competent department of environmental protection to be delivered.

The place of discharge of taxable pollutants as mentioned in Article 17 of the Environmental Protection Tax Law shall refer to:
(1) The location of the discharge outlet for taxable air pollutants and water pollutants;
(2) The place of generation of taxable solid waste;
(3) The place of generation of taxable noise.

Article 22 The abnormal tax declaration data and materials of a taxpayer mentioned in Article 20, paragraph 2 of the Environmental Protection Tax Law includes, but is not limited to, the following circumstances:
(1) The amount of taxable pollutant emissions declared by the taxpayer in the current period is significantly lower than that of the same period in the previous year, without justifiable reasons;
(2) The amount of pollutant emissions per unit of product of the taxpayer is significantly lower than that of similar taxpayers, without justifiable reasons.
